#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171681 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/id #uuid "59510c92-d9be-57c4-bc6d-497107401785" :question/math-academy-id "e-10051" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=100$ and\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} = 100 \\cdot 0.64 = 64 > 5$, and\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 100 \\cdot (1-0.64) = 36 > 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.645) = 0.05\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n0.64 ± 1.645 \\cdot \\sqrt{(0.64(1 - 0.64))/(100)}; 0.64 ± 0.079\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171682 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/id #uuid "221d0cad-0cb8-5cdc-816e-19408a796d05" :question/math-academy-id "e-10054" :question/problem "A survey of a random sample of $1\\,500$ U.S. households reveals that $780$ of them own at least one dog. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one dog.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one dog, and some don't,\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\, 500$, and\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one dog can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,} = \\dfrac{780}{1\\, 500} = 0.52\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} =1\\,500 \\cdot 0.52 = 780 > 5$, and\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 1\\,500 \\cdot (1-0.52) = 720 > 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.025}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that\n\n$$\nz_{0.025}=1.96{:}\n$$\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.025}$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.960}$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n0.52 ± 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(0.52(1 - 0.52))/(1500)}; 0.52 ± 0.025\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820a5f1b-40bc-5b51-83cd-74e72b152309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65 ± 0.201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168808} {:answer/id #uuid "8c574c8c-ffcb-596a-86f7-2c17a7ec1049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65 ± 0.093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168809} {:answer/id #uuid "b5830626-811c-5f31-a770-d36a4e7e6cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65 ± 0.052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168810} {:answer/id #uuid "dba92f05-9a2c-5c93-b1fc-5b35b844653b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95 ± 0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168811} {:answer/id #uuid "587cec6c-a924-59ad-87aa-15967c86eaef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95 ± 0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168812}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fb8692a1-74f0-5ff4-a1e6-f0adf87799e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168807}] :question/id #uuid "9b09786c-003f-565a-9fd2-fe6264490f01" :question/math-academy-id "q-142715" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n = 100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $65%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$, where $Z∼N(0, 1)$.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a30fb77f-030b-5fb5-8599-5389e13855b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.24 ± 0.091" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168814} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c5a202-7c9e-5f90-adcd-0a0668db6b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16 ± 0.005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168815} {:answer/id #uuid "d6619d48-23d6-5b2d-80eb-23a38f4b6727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16 ± 0.017" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168816} {:answer/id #uuid "010d656e-d5e9-54b5-b49a-26e305a6c30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.24 ± 0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168817} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa7d13f-058c-5005-a0a5-efe46c7aae4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16 ± 0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168818}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8362fec0-95c3-51e3-8cbd-6c9d9645d33c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168813}] :question/id #uuid "42e7a19f-f4f3-5e6a-9979-741a2870cc54" :question/math-academy-id "q-142726" :question/problem "A survey of $150$ randomly selected students at a particular university showed that only $24$ select at least one math course. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the proportion of students from the entire university who select at least one math course. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0, 1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
